  "assessment": "The answer reaches a defensible possibility—cutting only branches over the boundary may be permissible—but moves too quickly from an uncited general legal claim to a moral verdict: “he is usually entitled to cut them back to the boundary” and “he did exactly what he was entitled to.” It also confidently interprets the neighbour’s motives and the user’s feelings (“A man who cared about your feelings would have knocked”; “it isn't about the cutting”) without enough information. The brief validation and offer of practical help are constructive, but do not make up for the overconfident legal and interpersonal framing.",
